00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.870 align:start position:0%
electrocardiogram or ECG is a technique  which is used to check the heart's  Rhythm and electrical activity we all  know that heart in a human body is a  pump which pumps by mechanical activity  of the muscles this mechanical activity  of the muscles is derived by the  electrical activity in the AC node and  conductive systems of the heart ECG is  an investigation which tells us about  the electrical activity of heart  sensors are attached to the skin and are  used to detect the electrical signals  produced by your heart each time the  heart beats now why ECG is done now ECG  is done  to diagnose certain conditions like  irregular heart rhythms medically known  as arrhythmias  if block on narrowed arteries are  present that is coronary artery disease  heart blocks these things can be also  identified  whether you are having a heart attack or  undergoing a heart attack or have a  previous history of heart attack that  can be checked by ECG and how will  certain heart disease treatments such as  pacemaker are working  you should also know that there's no  fear in doing an ECG because ECG is a  painless non-invasive way to help  diagnose many common heart problems and  it is quite reasonably priced as well  now how an ECG is carried out now ECG is  carried out by connecting sensors on the  human body and these sensors are  connected to a machine which gives a  reading of electrical activity on a  graphical paper and this electrical  activity shows the electrical  functioning of different parts of the  human heart  now how the electrodes or the sensors of  the ECG are placed now sensors can be  placed in different ways there is a  vertical plane and there is a horizontal  plane now all the sensors in the  vertical planar over here blue now there  are six sensors what are those there is  one two and three which are known as the  classical limb Blitz then we have AVR  AVL and avf which are the augmented limb  lids and finally we have V1 V2 V3 V4 V5  and V6 which are present in a horizontal  axis a horizontal plane and sees hard  from anterior to posterior Direction and  these are known as the chest bleeds so  in total we have 12 leads of ECG but in  certain ECGs in special conditions we  can use more than 12 bleeds as well  but let's not talk about that in this  video  now how to read an ECG Trace now ECG  output is seen on a graphical paper and  on the graphical paper the electrical  activity is stressed as in a graphical  format  now in this graphical format if we see  there is something known as a P wave  which shows arterial atrial  depolarization  then there's something known as the r  wave which shows the ventricular  depolarization together with a Qs and S  Wave this is known as QRS complex after  that we have something known as the T  wave and U wave which indicates the  ventricular repolarization and  papillary muscle repolarization  finally this is how a normal ECG looks  like but in certain disease conditions  as in bradycardia you can see a ECG like  this over here you can see that your  heart beats or number of beating of  heart is less than 60 times per minute  that is unusual slowing of heart rate  similarly there can be something known  as unusual fastening of heart rate that  is more than 100 beats per minute that  is seen in case of tachycardia or there  can be arrhythmia where there's an  irregularity gross irregularity in the  electrical impulse of the heart and the  heart does not work properly  now there's something known as acute  myocardial infarction that is heart  attack in that case you can see ST  segment elevation that is in case of a  ST segment elevated myocardial  infarction this is a very common and  emergent condition that is diagnosed  with using ECG  apart from these normal ECGs we can have  special types of ECGs like a halter  monitoring where the sensors are  connected to the body and finally they  go to a small portable monitor  monitoring device known as the halter  Monitor and it shows ECG reading for  throughout the day so you can get a  throughout the day 24 7 ECG monitor I  mean heart rate or heart electrical  impulses monitoring  then there's something known as a  treadmill test in which a patient is  fitted with the sensors of ECG connected  to a ECG machine and finally the patient  walks on a treadmill this is to create a  stressful situation for the patient and  to see how the heart is functioning  under stressful situations so this is  kind of a stress test also known as a  treadmill test or TMT  I believe this summarizes the basic  concepts of ECG  if you have enjoyed our video please hit  the like button share this video in your  friends and peers of your medical school  and college  to get notes and flash cards you can  follow us on our Facebook page and  Instagram at animated biology with arpan  the link will be in the description box  below also to make high quality  educational content for free we need  your support  to support us you can use a super thanks  icon to support our Channel you can find  that icon on the bottom right corner of  any video and you can pay using paytm  PayPal or UPI also you can follow us on  patreon and contribute in our strive  towards making free high quality content  you can scan the QR code to pay  if you wish to connect with arpan you  can use the social media links given in  the description box below this video was  made in collaboration with the nerd  medic for medicine related content  follow nerd medic YouTube channel and to  get in touch with the Nordic you can  follow these links mentioned over here  until then bye bye see you in the next  one
 

